WHY IT CANNOT WORK HERE, read from the aligned view afterwards: the law's mechanism needs an `addi c,p,K` for the peephole to fuse into the load. Our build never materialises one - it folds the +240 straight into the load DISPLACEMENT (`lfs f0,240(r7)`), so there is no address instruction to fuse and both spellings compile to the same bytes. The target's `lfsu f0,240(r8)` is a write-back whose result is dead (r8 is recomputed as `add r8,r4,r6` on the next iteration), i.e. the target chose the update form gratuitously. That is an allocator choice downstream of a value graph our source already matches.

PROBE 2 - the three li/mr zero-init pairs at +0xf9c/+0xfac, +0x1574/+0x1584 and +0x1678/+0x1688. v1 recorded one statement-reorder spelling as NEUTRAL and left 'a respell of the remaining two' as untried. All three sites are ONE shape, now decoded: target emits `li r0,<divisor>; li rX,0; <two lwz>; subf; divwu; li r3,0` - one zero BEFORE the division and one AFTER - while ours emits both zeros first, CSEs the constant into one `li` and copies with `mr`. The four source sites sharing the shape are L516/L526/L565/L584/L602 (`count = (...)/N; n = 0; off = 0;`). Probed the confirmed 108-divisor site (L526 = sizeof(struct camera_data)) in isolation:
-                    count = (u32)((u8*)gWorldData->sounds -
-                                  (u8*)gWorldData->cameras) / sizeof(struct camera_data);
-                    n = 0;
                     off = 0;
+                    n = 0;
+                    count = (u32)((u8*)gWorldData->sounds -
+                                  (u8*)gWorldData->cameras) / sizeof(struct camera_data);
+                    off = 0;
Byte-identical object. MWCC's constant CSE runs after statement scheduling and does not see source statement order, so no permutation of the two zero-inits can split the constant into two `li`s.
